    "q-319944": {
      "dom_id": "step-q319944",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "The entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ k & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nequals $-2.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ k & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ k & -1\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ k & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)((-1)(-1)-(-2)(-2))+2((-2)(-1)-(-2)k)+(-2)((-2)(-2)-(-1)k) \\\\  & =(-2)(1-4)+2(2+2k)+(-2)(4+k) \\\\  & =6+4+4k-8-2k \\\\  & =2k+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{32}^{t}={c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ k & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-((-2)(-2)-(-2)k) \\\\  & =-(4+2k) \\\\  & =-2k-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{32}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{23} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2k-4}{2k+2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k+2}{k+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{32}^{′}=-2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{k+2}{k+1} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{k+2}{k+1} & =2 \\\\ k+2 & =2k+2 \\\\ k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
