    "q-104435": {
      "dom_id": "step-q104435",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "The entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & k & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $2.$ Find the value of $k.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & k & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}k & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}2 & k \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-k-6)-0+1(6-k) \\\\  & =-k-6+6-k \\\\  & =-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-2-2) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2k}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{21}^{′}=2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{k} & =2 \\\\ -2 & =2k \\\\ -\\frac{2}{2} & =k \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
